    {
      "id": "KA-S132-sign-visarga",
      "sequence": 1051,
      "title": "◌ಃ (visarga) — one character, two dots, a breath at the end",
      "headword": "ಃ",
      "romanization": "visarga",
      "gloss": "the single character ಃ — two dots of breath, the other mark that rides at the end of a syllable",
      "script": "kannada",
      "modality": "pen",
      "derivedModality": "pen",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "writing-type",
        "writing-block",
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d18152eaea479fa3",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "pen",
        "needs": [
          "a pen and something to write on",
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "Script you'll notice: ◌ಃ",
          "Guided Practice"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your hands, so it is not a driving lesson. You will want a pen and something to write on and your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the sections called Script you'll notice: ◌ಃ and Guided Practice until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: ◌ೃ — where does it sit, and which standing letter is it the other half of?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One character this time. It has a partner you met long ago, and the two are easiest to learn side by side."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "Script you'll notice: ◌ಃ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "◌ಃ (visarga) — two dots, one above the other, sitting after a syllable."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada has two marks that ride at the end of a syllable rather than standing as letters. You already read one:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "◌ಂ — one small circle. Turns the end of the syllable nasal, the sound in the middle of ಸಂತೋಷ santōṣa."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "◌ಃ (visarga) — two dots. Adds a breath, an h let out after the vowel."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say santōṣa and feel the air go up into your nose on the ◌ಂ. Now say ah and let it fall away as a soft ha. That falling breath is what the two dots are for."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hold the two marks as a pair: neither is a letter you can sound out alone, and both attach to what came before them."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Where you will meet it. Rarely, and almost always ending a word Kannada borrowed from Sanskrit. Knowing the two dots on sight is enough."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "writing",
          "title": "Writing: ◌ಃ — copy what you see",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put your pen on ◌ಃ (visarga) and follow its line. Copy the shape you can see — slowly, and larger than it is printed."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This book does not yet tell you where to start the character or which way to travel. That is a real thing, taught with real variation from school to school, and it is not written down here until it can be written down with a source. Copying what is in front of you needs no such source, and it is how the shape gets into your hand in the meantime."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "LOOK",
              "instruction": "at these two marks and say which one turns the syllable nasal",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU LOOK: at these two marks and say which one turns the syllable nasal]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "◌ಂ, ◌ಃ (visarga)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "TRACE",
              "instruction": "◌ಃ (visarga) three times, letting a soft breath out as you finish each one",
              "spoken": false,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU TRACE: ◌ಃ three times, letting a soft breath out as you finish each one]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "LOOK",
              "instruction": "at ಸಂತೋಷ and find the other mark inside it",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU LOOK: at ಸಂತೋಷ and find the other mark inside it]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the nasal one, then the breathed one, and name what each adds",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the nasal one, then the breathed one, and name what each adds]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which character is this — ◌ಃ (visarga), and what does it add to the syllable before it? (A breath, an h after the vowel.) Which mark is its partner, and what does that one add? (◌ಂ, a nasal.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
